{
  "dom_id": "step-q109241",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14133198/assets/525bd983bacfce2de8664b92947b53bdcc48d1d28073ffaa4becc03d23f6594d.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x={e}^{y}$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y})={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{1+{({e}^{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
